// A TEACHING MAP FOR CONTRAPOSITION, in the reconstructor's own words (17 Oct 2026). It sets out
// the exchange at the end of G. E. Moore's "Proof of an External World" (1939) without quoting it:
// the text is in copyright in the UK until 2029, so the quoted map stays private. Open it, deny
// the conclusion of either argument, and keep what each side grants: the page then says which
// premise must go. Kept by app/test_suppose.mjs.
//
// One person's modus ponens is another's modus tollens. The sceptic runs the argument forward from
// "if you cannot prove it, you do not know it". Moore keeps two of its premisses, denies its
// conclusion, and so denies that first premiss. Deny the conclusion of Moore's proof instead, keep
// what hands are, and the page forces his premiss: the move an idealist has to make.
===
title: "Teaching -- one person's modus ponens"
===

# Moore's proof {isGroup: true}

[External things exist]: At least two things outside our minds exist now.

<Moore's proof>: Holding up his hands, Moore proves that external things exist.

(1) [Here are two hands]: Here is one hand, and here is another.
    {formalization: "h"}
(2) [Hands are external]: If here are two hands, then at least two things outside our minds exist.
    {formalization: "h -> e"}
-- Modus ponens --
(3) [External things exist]
    {formalization: "e"}

# The sceptic's reply {isGroup: true}

<The sceptic's argument>: If Moore cannot prove his premiss he does not know it; if he does not know it, his proof proves nothing. #reported

(1) [No proof, no knowledge]: If Moore cannot prove that here are two hands, he does not know it. #reported
    {formalization: "-p -> -k"}
(2) [Moore cannot prove it]: Moore cannot prove that here are two hands: he cannot prove he is not dreaming. #conceded
    {formalization: "-p"}
-- Modus ponens {uses: [1, 2]} --
(3) [Moore does not know it]: Moore does not know that here are two hands. #reported
    {formalization: "-k"}
(4) [Knowledge needed]: If Moore does not know his premiss, his proof proves nothing. #conceded
    {formalization: "-k -> -c"}
-- Modus ponens {uses: [3, 4]} --
(5) [The proof proves nothing]: Moore's proof proves nothing. #reported
    {formalization: "-c"}

[The proof proves nothing]
    -> [External things exist]

# Moore's answer {isGroup: true}

[Knowledge without proof]: Moore can know things he cannot prove, and he knew the premisses of his proof.
    >< [No proof, no knowledge]
